# Line endings, decided here rather than by whatever each machine's Git was
# installed with.
#
# The problem this fixes, which bit repeatedly: Git for Windows ships with
# core.autocrlf=true, so a clone made on Windows rewrites every text file to
# CRLF on checkout. The repo's own files are all LF and stay that way -- but
# this project is *worked on from WSL against a checkout living on a Windows
# drive*, and bash cannot run a CRLF script at all:
#
#     start.sh: line 8: $'\r': command not found
#     : invalid option name: pipefail
#
# It is not a warning either. A CRLF script dies at its first `if`, because
# `then\r` is not `then` -- so no guard written in shell can survive its own
# file, and there is no version of a .sh file that works both ways. The only
# fix is for CRLF never to reach one.
#
# .gitattributes beats core.autocrlf and core.eol, and it is committed, so
# this holds on every clone on every machine without anyone configuring
# anything.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository, and check it out as LF.
# Only the batch files below want anything else.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception and genuinely want CRLF: cmd.exe is
# documented to mis-handle LF-only files around labels and goto, and
# START.bat is built out of subroutines it jumps between. See the batch trap
# in CLAUDE.md for how expensive a cmd.exe parsing surprise already was here.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Never touched. `text=auto` already detects these by their NUL bytes, but
# saying so is cheap and the cost of being wrong is not: a CRLF pass over
# static/logo.png would corrupt the alpha channel the printed card composites
# against, and over static/fonts/*.ttf the typefaces every card is set in.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.db    binary
*.exe   binary
*.zip   binary
